            <formatted-text>
              <run fontsize='8'>Key finding: Contrary to the disparity hypothesis, minority-serving hospitals  (≥50% minority share) receive higher average payments per beneficiary ($24,495)  than low-minority hospitals ($16,707). This is explained by urban concentration - minority-serving hospitals cluster in high-cost cities where CMS wage index adjustments increase reimbursements. Geography, not race, drives this payment gap.</run>
              <run bold='true' fontsize='8'>Data note: Analysis at hospital level. Patient-level relationships may differ.</run>
            </formatted-text>
            <zone-style>
              <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
              <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
              <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
              <format attr='margin' value='4' />
            </zone-style>
